const ORIG_QUESTIONS = [{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which type of acute kidney injury is caused by decreased blood flow to the kidneys?","opts":["Prerenal AKI","Intrarenal AKI","Postrenal AKI","Chronic AKI"],"ans":0,"expl":"Prerenal AKI results from hypoperfusion of the kidneys due to conditions like hypotension, heart failure, or volume depletion."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What is the hallmark finding in acute tubular necrosis (ATN)?","opts":["Proteinuria","Muddy brown casts in urine","Hematuria","Glucosuria"],"ans":1,"expl":"Muddy brown granular casts are characteristic of ATN, indicating tubular epithelial cell damage and sloughing."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which stage of CKD corresponds to a GFR of 15-29 mL/min/1.73m²?","opts":["Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4","Stage 5"],"ans":2,"expl":"CKD Stage 4 is defined by GFR 15-29 mL/min/1.73m², indicating severe kidney damage and preparation for renal replacement therapy."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What electrolyte abnormality is most commonly associated with advanced CKD?","opts":["Hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypophosphatemia"],"ans":0,"expl":"Hyperkalemia occurs in advanced CKD due to decreased potassium excretion by damaged nephrons."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which condition is characterized by heavy proteinuria (>3.5g/day), hypoalbuminemia, and edema?","opts":["Nephritic syndrome","Nephrotic syndrome","Acute glomerulonephritis","Pyelonephritis"],"ans":1,"expl":"Nephrotic syndrome presents with massive proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia due to glomerular permeability changes."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What is the primary cause of postrenal AKI?","opts":["Glomerulonephritis","Urinary tract obstruction","Acute tubular necrosis","Renal artery stenosis"],"ans":1,"expl":"Postrenal AKI results from obstruction of urine flow, such as from kidney stones, tumors, or prostate enlargement."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which medication class should be used cautiously in patients with CKD due to nephrotoxicity risk?","opts":["ACE inhibitors","NSAIDs","Beta-blockers","Calcium channel blockers"],"ans":1,"expl":"NSAIDs can cause vasoconstriction of afferent arterioles and worsen kidney function, especially in CKD patients."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What is the most common cause of glomerulonephritis worldwide?","opts":["Diabetic nephropathy","IgA nephropathy","Lupus nephritis","Post-streptococcal GN"],"ans":1,"expl":"IgA nephropathy (Berger disease) is the most common primary glomerulonephritis globally."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which finding distinguishes nephritic from nephrotic syndrome?","opts":["Proteinuria","Hematuria with RBC casts","Edema","Hypoalbuminemia"],"ans":1,"expl":"Nephritic syndrome is characterized by hematuria with RBC casts, hypertension, and mild-to-moderate proteinuria."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What is the target blood pressure for most patients with CKD and proteinuria?","opts":["<140/90 mmHg","<130/80 mmHg","<120/70 mmHg","<150/90 mmHg"],"ans":1,"expl":"Current guidelines recommend BP <130/80 mmHg for CKD patients, especially those with proteinuria."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which hormone deficiency contributes to anemia in CKD?","opts":["Insulin","Erythropoietin","Aldosterone","Parathyroid hormone"],"ans":1,"expl":"Damaged kidneys produce less erythropoietin, leading to decreased red blood cell production and anemia."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What is the primary treatment for hyperkalemia in a patient with peaked T waves on ECG?","opts":["Oral potassium binders","IV calcium gluconate","Dietary restriction","Loop diuretics"],"ans":1,"expl":"IV calcium gluconate stabilizes cardiac membranes immediately in severe hyperkalemia with ECG changes."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which type of dialysis uses the peritoneal membrane as a filter?","opts":["Hemodialysis","Peritoneal dialysis","Continuous venovenous hemofiltration","Plasmapheresis"],"ans":1,"expl":"Peritoneal dialysis uses the peritoneal membrane for solute and fluid exchange via instilled dialysate."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What is the most accurate indicator of kidney function?","opts":["Serum creatinine","Blood urea nitrogen","Glomerular filtration rate (GFR)","Urine output"],"ans":2,"expl":"GFR is the best overall measure of kidney function, estimating filtration capacity per minute."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which condition is a common cause of prerenal AKI in hospitalized patients?","opts":["Sepsis","Glomerulonephritis","Kidney stones","Polycystic kidney disease"],"ans":0,"expl":"Sepsis causes vasodilation and hypotension, leading to renal hypoperfusion and prerenal AKI."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What dietary modification is typically recommended for CKD patients with hyperphosphatemia?","opts":["High protein diet","Phosphate restriction","High potassium diet","Low calcium intake"],"ans":1,"expl":"Phosphate restriction and phosphate binders help control hyperphosphatemia in CKD."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which finding suggests acute glomerulonephritis?","opts":["Muddy brown casts","RBC casts and dysmorphic RBCs","WBC casts","Hyaline casts"],"ans":1,"expl":"RBC casts and dysmorphic red blood cells indicate glomerular bleeding, characteristic of glomerulonephritis."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What is the definition of oliguria in adults?","opts":["<500 mL/day","<400 mL/day","<300 mL/day","<100 mL/day"],"ans":1,"expl":"Oliguria is defined as urine output less than 400 mL per day in adults."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"Which complication is associated with rapid correction of hyponatremia in CKD patients?","opts":["Cerebral edema","Osmotic demyelination syndrome","Cardiac arrest","Seizures"],"ans":1,"expl":"Rapid sodium correction can cause osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis)."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","q":"What is the primary mechanism of metabolic acidosis in CKD?","opts":["Increased acid production","Decreased acid excretion","Bicarbonate overproduction","Respiratory compensation"],"ans":1,"expl":"CKD impairs the kidney's ability to excrete hydrogen ions and regenerate bicarbonate, causing metabolic acidosis."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the primary mechanism by which solutes move across a dialyzer membrane during hemodialysis?","opts":["Ultrafiltration","Diffusion","Convection","Osmosis"],"ans":1,"expl":"Diffusion is the primary mechanism of solute clearance in hemodialysis, where solutes move from an area of higher concentration (blood) to lower concentration (dialysate) across a semipermeable membrane."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which vascular access type has the lowest risk of infection and longest patency for chronic hemodialysis?","opts":["Tunneled dialysis catheter","Arteriovenous graft","Arteriovenous fistula","Peripheral IV line"],"ans":2,"expl":"Arteriovenous fistulas (AVF) have the lowest infection rate, best long-term patency, and are considered the gold standard for chronic hemodialysis access."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What does Kt/V measure in hemodialysis?","opts":["Blood flow rate","Dialysis adequacy","Membrane permeability","Ultrafiltration volume"],"ans":1,"expl":"Kt/V is a dimensionless number used to quantify hemodialysis adequacy, where K is dialyzer clearance, t is treatment time, and V is volume of distribution of urea."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"During hemodialysis, ultrafiltration primarily removes which of the following?","opts":["Large proteins","Excess fluid","Red blood cells","Glucose"],"ans":1,"expl":"Ultrafiltration is the process of removing excess fluid from the blood by applying hydrostatic pressure across the dialyzer membrane, forcing water and small solutes through."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which anticoagulant is most commonly used during routine hemodialysis treatments?","opts":["Warfarin","Heparin","Aspirin","Clopidogrel"],"ans":1,"expl":"Unfractionated heparin is the most commonly used anticoagulant during hemodialysis to prevent clotting in the extracorporeal circuit."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the most common intradialytic complication during hemodialysis?","opts":["Air embolism","Hypotension","Seizures","Hemolysis"],"ans":1,"expl":"Intradialytic hypotension is the most frequent complication, occurring in 20-30% of treatments, often due to rapid fluid removal or autonomic dysfunction."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which dialysate component is typically set at 135-145 mEq/L?","opts":["Potassium","Calcium","Sodium","Bicarbonate"],"ans":2,"expl":"Dialysate sodium concentration is typically maintained between 135-145 mEq/L to match physiologic serum sodium levels and prevent osmotic shifts."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is convection in the context of hemodialysis?","opts":["Solute movement due to concentration gradient","Solute movement with water flow across membrane","Movement of water only","Movement of proteins"],"ans":1,"expl":"Convection is the movement of solutes across the membrane along with water flow (solvent drag), particularly important for middle molecule clearance."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which vascular access requires the longest maturation time before use?","opts":["Tunneled catheter","Arteriovenous graft","Arteriovenous fistula","Non-tunneled catheter"],"ans":2,"expl":"Arteriovenous fistulas typically require 3-6 months to mature before they can be safely used for dialysis, longer than grafts or catheters."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the recommended minimum Kt/V value for adequate hemodialysis?","opts":["0.8","1.2","2.0","3.5"],"ans":1,"expl":"KDOQI guidelines recommend a minimum single-pool Kt/V of 1.2 per dialysis session to ensure adequate dialysis delivery."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which symptom is most characteristic of dialysis disequilibrium syndrome?","opts":["Hypertension","Neurological symptoms including headache and confusion","Muscle cramps","Fever"],"ans":1,"expl":"Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome presents with neurological symptoms (headache, nausea, confusion, seizures) due to rapid solute removal causing cerebral edema."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the primary cause of muscle cramps during hemodialysis?","opts":["Hyperkalemia","Rapid ultrafiltration and volume depletion","High dialysate calcium","Excessive anticoagulation"],"ans":1,"expl":"Muscle cramps during dialysis are commonly caused by rapid fluid removal leading to volume depletion and changes in muscle perfusion."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which air embolism symptom would appear FIRST if air enters the venous line?","opts":["Chest pain and dyspnea","Bradycardia","Hypertension","Fever"],"ans":0,"expl":"Air embolism typically presents first with sudden chest pain, dyspnea, and cough as air reaches the pulmonary circulation."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the function of the dialyzer membrane's pore size?","opts":["Determines blood flow rate","Controls which molecules can pass through","Sets dialysate temperature","Regulates anticoagulation"],"ans":1,"expl":"Membrane pore size determines the molecular weight cutoff, controlling which solutes can be cleared while retaining larger proteins like albumin."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which dialysate temperature is typically used to reduce intradialytic hypotension?","opts":["39°C","35-36°C (cool dialysate)","42°C","Room temperature"],"ans":1,"expl":"Cool dialysate (35-36°C) promotes peripheral vasoconstriction and improves hemodynamic stability, reducing hypotension episodes."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the primary advantage of high-flux dialyzers?","opts":["Lower cost","Better clearance of middle molecules","Reduced anticoagulation needs","Shorter treatment time"],"ans":1,"expl":"High-flux dialyzers have larger pores that allow better clearance of middle molecular weight toxins like beta-2 microglobulin."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which access complication is characterized by a thrill that is weak or absent?","opts":["Infection","Thrombosis","Aneurysm formation","Steal syndrome"],"ans":1,"expl":"A weak or absent thrill suggests thrombosis or stenosis of the vascular access, requiring immediate evaluation."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the purpose of bicarbonate in the dialysate?","opts":["To lower blood pressure","To correct metabolic acidosis","To prevent clotting","To reduce potassium"],"ans":1,"expl":"Bicarbonate in dialysate helps correct the metabolic acidosis common in ESRD patients by diffusing into the blood and buffering acids."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"Which patient position is recommended if an air embolism is suspected?","opts":["Supine flat","Left lateral decubitus with head down","Sitting upright","Prone"],"ans":1,"expl":"Left lateral decubitus (Durant's maneuver) with head down traps air in the right ventricle, preventing it from entering pulmonary circulation."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","q":"What is the typical blood flow rate range for maintenance hemodialysis?","opts":["50-100 mL/min","200-500 mL/min","600-800 mL/min","1000-1200 mL/min"],"ans":1,"expl":"Standard blood flow rates for hemodialysis range from 200-500 mL/min, with 300-400 mL/min being most common for adequate clearance."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which peritoneal dialysis catheter type is placed using a laparoscopic approach and has a pre-sternal exit site?","opts":["Tenckhoff catheter","Presternal catheter","Moncrief-Popovich catheter","Fluoroscopically placed catheter"],"ans":1,"expl":"Presternal catheters are surgically placed with the exit site on the chest wall, beneficial for patients with abdominal ostomies or obesity."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What is the recommended frequency for exit-site care in stable peritoneal dialysis patients?","opts":["Daily","Weekly","After each exchange","Only when drainage is noted"],"ans":1,"expl":"Exit-site care is typically performed weekly in stable patients, though frequency may increase if drainage or infection is present."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which organism is the most common cause of peritoneal dialysis-associated peritonitis?","opts":["Escherichia coli","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Candida albicans"],"ans":1,"expl":"Coagulase-negative staphylococci, particularly S. epidermidis, are the most frequent pathogens in PD peritonitis due to skin flora contamination."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What does a high transporter status on the Peritoneal Equilibration Test (PET) indicate?","opts":["Slow solute clearance requiring longer dwells","Rapid solute equilibration with poor ultrafiltration on long dwells","Normal membrane function","Membrane failure requiring transplant"],"ans":1,"expl":"High transporters rapidly equilibrate solutes but lose osmotic gradient quickly, leading to poor UF on long dwells; they benefit from shorter cycles."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which dialysate solution is preferred for patients with hyperkalemia?","opts":["Standard lactate-buffered solution","Potassium-free solution","Bicarbonate-buffered solution","Icodextrin solution"],"ans":1,"expl":"Potassium-free dialysate helps correct hyperkalemia by promoting potassium removal across the peritoneal membrane."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What is the primary mechanism of ultrafiltration in peritoneal dialysis?","opts":["Diffusion","Osmosis","Convection","Active transport"],"ans":1,"expl":"Ultrafiltration occurs primarily through osmosis, driven by the osmotic gradient created by glucose or icodextrin in the dialysate."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"CAPD stands for:","opts":["Continuous Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis","Cyclic Automated Peritoneal Dialysis","Chronic Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis","Continuous Automated Peritoneal Dialysis"],"ans":0,"expl":"CAPD is Continuous Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis, performed manually throughout the day without a machine."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which symptom is most indicative of peritonitis in a PD patient?","opts":["Mild abdominal discomfort","Cloudy effluent with abdominal pain","Increased ultrafiltration volume","Decreased appetite alone"],"ans":1,"expl":"Cloudy effluent combined with abdominal pain is the hallmark presentation of peritonitis and requires immediate evaluation."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What is the first-line empiric antibiotic coverage for PD peritonitis?","opts":["Gram-positive only","Gram-negative only","Both Gram-positive and Gram-negative","Antifungal only"],"ans":2,"expl":"Empiric therapy must cover both Gram-positive (e.g., vancomycin or cephalosporin) and Gram-negative organisms (e.g., ceftazidime or aminoglycoside)."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Ultrafiltration failure is defined as:","opts":["Inability to achieve target solute clearance","Persistent inability to remove adequate fluid volume","Complete loss of peritoneal membrane function","Catheter mechanical failure"],"ans":1,"expl":"UF failure is the inability to remove sufficient fluid despite appropriate osmotic agents, often due to membrane changes or high transporter status."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which nutrient requires careful monitoring in PD patients due to glucose absorption from dialysate?","opts":["Protein","Potassium","Calories/carbohydrates","Vitamin D"],"ans":2,"expl":"Significant glucose absorption from dialysate can contribute 100-300+ calories daily, affecting glycemic control and weight."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What is the recommended protein intake for stable PD patients?","opts":["0.6-0.8 g/kg/day","1.0-1.2 g/kg/day","1.5-2.0 g/kg/day","No restriction needed"],"ans":1,"expl":"PD patients need 1.0-1.2 g/kg/day due to protein losses in dialysate (5-15g/day) and catabolic state of uremia."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which PD modality uses a cycler machine primarily during nighttime sleep?","opts":["CAPD","CCPD","Tidal PD","Intermittent PD"],"ans":1,"expl":"CCPD (Continuous Cyclic Peritoneal Dialysis) uses a cycler at night with a daytime dwell, offering more freedom during waking hours."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What is the purpose of the Peritoneal Equilibration Test (PET)?","opts":["To measure residual kidney function","To classify peritoneal membrane transport characteristics","To diagnose peritonitis","To assess catheter patency"],"ans":1,"expl":"PET classifies patients as high, high-average, low-average, or low transporters to guide prescription optimization."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which catheter complication presents as leakage of dialysate around the exit site?","opts":["Catheter migration","Exit-site infection","Pericatheter leak","Omental wrapping"],"ans":2,"expl":"Pericatheter leaks occur early post-placement or from increased intra-abdominal pressure, causing fluid to track around the catheter."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Icodextrin solution is particularly useful for:","opts":["Patients with peritonitis","Long daytime dwells in high transporters","Emergency hyperkalemia treatment","Patients with fungal infections"],"ans":1,"expl":"Icodextrin provides sustained osmotic gradient for long dwells, ideal for high transporters who lose glucose-based UF quickly."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What is the minimum dwell time typically recommended for adequate exchange in CAPD?","opts":["15 minutes","30 minutes","2-4 hours","8 hours"],"ans":2,"expl":"Standard CAPD exchanges use 2-4 hour dwells to balance solute clearance and ultrafiltration efficiency."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Which finding suggests catheter tip migration on imaging?","opts":["Catheter tip in the pelvis","Catheter tip in the upper abdomen or subphrenic space","Cuff visible at exit site","Normal catheter trajectory"],"ans":1,"expl":"Migration to the upper abdomen can cause inflow pain and drainage problems; the tip should rest in the pelvis."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"Encapsulating Peritoneal Sclerosis (EPS) is characterized by:","opts":["Reversible membrane inflammation","Fibrotic encapsulation of bowel with obstruction","Temporary UF failure","Mild exit-site infection"],"ans":1,"expl":"EPS is a rare but serious complication with thick fibrous membrane formation around intestines, causing obstruction and requiring surgical intervention."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","q":"What is the role of heparin in PD dialysate?","opts":["Anticoagulation for systemic effect","Prevention of fibrin clot formation in catheter","Treatment of peritonitis","Enhancement of ultrafiltration"],"ans":1,"expl":"Heparin (500-1000 units/L) prevents fibrin formation in the catheter or effluent without causing systemic anticoagulation."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which type of kidney donor typically provides the best long-term graft survival?","opts":["Living related donor","Deceased donor after brain death","Deceased donor after circulatory death","Living unrelated donor"],"ans":0,"expl":"Living related donors generally provide the best outcomes due to better HLA matching, shorter cold ischemia time, and the ability to schedule transplantation electively before the recipient requires dialysis."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is the primary mechanism of hyperacute rejection?","opts":["T-cell mediated cytotoxicity","Pre-formed antibodies against donor HLA antigens","Chronic antibody-mediated injury","Drug toxicity from calcineurin inhibitors"],"ans":1,"expl":"Hyperacute rejection occurs within minutes to hours due to pre-existing antibodies in the recipient that react against donor antigens, causing immediate thrombosis and graft necrosis. This is why crossmatch testing is critical before transplantation."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which immunosuppressive medication is most commonly associated with nephrotoxicity?","opts":["Mycophenolate mofetil","Prednisone","Tacrolimus","Sirolimus"],"ans":2,"expl":"Tacrolimus and other calcineurin inhibitors cause dose-dependent nephrotoxicity through vasoconstriction of afferent arterioles. Therapeutic drug monitoring is essential to balance rejection prevention with kidney protection."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is the typical time frame for acute cellular rejection to occur post-transplant?","opts":["Within 24 hours","Days to weeks, most common in first 3-6 months","After 5 years","Only during pregnancy"],"ans":1,"expl":"Acute cellular rejection most commonly occurs within the first 3-6 months post-transplant when immunosuppression levels may be subtherapeutic or during medication adjustments. It can occur at any time with inadequate immunosuppression."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which virus is most concerning for post-transplant infection in seronegative recipients receiving organs from seropositive donors?","opts":["Hepatitis A virus","Cytomegalovirus (CMV)","Rhinovirus","Parvovirus B19"],"ans":1,"expl":"CMV D+/R- (donor positive, recipient negative) represents the highest risk scenario. CMV can cause direct tissue invasion and indirect effects including increased rejection risk and opportunistic infections. Prophylaxis is standard for high-risk patients."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is the primary purpose of induction therapy in kidney transplantation?","opts":["To treat established rejection","To provide intense immunosuppression during the highest risk period immediately post-transplant","To replace maintenance immunosuppression","To prevent viral infections"],"ans":1,"expl":"Induction therapy uses potent immunosuppressive agents (such as basiliximab or antithymocyte globulin) during the immediate post-transplant period when rejection risk is highest, allowing delayed introduction or lower doses of nephrotoxic maintenance agents."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"BK virus nephropathy primarily affects which part of the transplanted kidney?","opts":["Glomeruli only","Renal tubular epithelial cells","Blood vessels only","Collecting system only"],"ans":1,"expl":"BK virus infects and replicates in renal tubular epithelial cells, causing interstitial nephritis and potential graft loss. Screening with urine decoy cells or plasma BK viral load is standard in the first year post-transplant."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which prophylactic medication is typically given to prevent Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PJP) in transplant recipients?","opts":["Acyclovir","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Fluconazole","Isoniazid"],"ans":1,"expl":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX) is the first-line prophylaxis for PJP, typically given for 6-12 months post-transplant. It also provides protection against other opportunistic infections including Toxoplasma and some bacterial infections."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is the hallmark histological finding of chronic antibody-mediated rejection?","opts":["Tubular necrosis","Transplant glomerulopathy with double contours","Acute tubular injury","Glomerular hyperfiltration"],"ans":1,"expl":"Chronic antibody-mediated rejection is characterized by transplant glomerulopathy (duplication of glomerular basement membranes), interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy, and often C4d deposition in peritubular capillaries."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which factor is an absolute contraindication to kidney transplantation?","opts":["Age over 70 years","Active malignancy","Controlled hypertension","Type 2 diabetes"],"ans":1,"expl":"Active malignancy is generally an absolute contraindication due to the risk of cancer progression with immunosuppression. Most centers require a cancer-free period (typically 2-5 years depending on cancer type) before listing."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is the standard preservation solution used for deceased donor kidneys during cold storage?","opts":["Normal saline","University of Wisconsin (UW) solution","Lactated Ringer's","5% Dextrose"],"ans":1,"expl":"University of Wisconsin solution is the most commonly used preservation fluid, containing impermeants and substrates that minimize cellular swelling and injury during cold ischemia. It allows safe preservation for 24-48 hours."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which maintenance immunosuppression regimen is most commonly used in kidney transplantation?","opts":["Monotherapy with prednisone","Triple therapy: calcineurin inhibitor + antimetabolite + corticosteroid","Dual therapy with two calcineurin inhibitors","Steroid-only regimen"],"ans":1,"expl":"Triple therapy combining a calcineurin inhibitor (tacrolimus), antimetabolite (mycophenolate), and corticosteroid provides synergistic immunosuppression with different mechanisms, allowing lower doses of each agent and reduced toxicity."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is the most common cause of graft loss in the first year post-transplant?","opts":["Hyperacute rejection","Death with functioning graft","Acute rejection","Surgical complications"],"ans":1,"expl":"Death with a functioning graft has become the leading cause of graft loss, particularly from cardiovascular disease and infection. This reflects improved rejection prevention but ongoing challenges with long-term complications of immunosuppression."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which HLA locus is most important for kidney transplant matching?","opts":["HLA-DP","HLA-DR","HLA-A","HLA-C"],"ans":1,"expl":"HLA-DR matching has the strongest impact on graft survival, followed by HLA-B and HLA-A. Zero-antigen mismatch kidneys from deceased donors receive priority allocation due to superior outcomes."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is delayed graft function (DGF) defined as?","opts":["Need for dialysis within the first week post-transplant","Creatinine elevation after 6 months","Immediate urine output post-transplant","Biopsy-proven rejection"],"ans":0,"expl":"DGF is typically defined as the need for dialysis within the first week after transplantation. It is more common with deceased donor kidneys, prolonged cold ischemia time, and donor hemodynamic instability."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which medication requires therapeutic drug monitoring due to narrow therapeutic index and variable metabolism?","opts":["Prednisone","Mycophenolate mofetil","Tacrolimus","Dapsone"],"ans":2,"expl":"Tacrolimus has significant interpatient variability in metabolism via CYP3A4/5, requiring regular trough level monitoring. Target levels vary by time post-transplant and rejection risk."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is the primary mechanism of action of mycophenolate mofetil?","opts":["Calcineurin inhibition","Inhibition of inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase","mTOR inhibition","IL-2 receptor blockade"],"ans":1,"expl":"Mycophenolate inhibits IMPDH, blocking de novo purine synthesis. Lymphocytes depend on this pathway (unlike other cells that can use salvage pathways), providing selective immunosuppression."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which condition is a common metabolic complication of long-term corticosteroid use?","opts":["Hypoglycemia","Hyperglycemia and new-onset diabetes","Hypotension","Weight loss"],"ans":1,"expl":"Corticosteroids cause insulin resistance, increased hepatic gluconeogenesis, and can lead to post-transplant diabetes mellitus (PTDM). They also contribute to weight gain, dyslipidemia, and bone disease."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"What is the recommended screening interval for BK virus in the first year post-transplant?","opts":["Weekly","Monthly for first 3-6 months, then every 3 months","Annually","Only if symptoms develop"],"ans":1,"expl":"Standard screening involves monthly plasma BK viral load or urine decoy cell testing for the first 3-6 months, then every 3 months through year one. Early detection allows immunosuppression reduction before nephropathy develops."},{"topic":"Transplant","q":"Which type of rejection is primarily mediated by donor-specific antibodies?","opts":["Acute cellular rejection","Antibody-mediated rejection","Interstitial fibrosis","Drug toxicity"],"ans":1,"expl":"Antibody-mediated rejection (AMR) is caused by donor-specific antibodies binding to graft endothelium, activating complement and causing injury. It can be acute or chronic and is diagnosed by histology, C4d staining, and DSA detection."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"Which solute removal mechanism in CRRT relies primarily on hydrostatic pressure to move fluid and dissolved solutes across a semipermeable membrane?","opts":["Diffusion","Convection","Adsorption","Ultrafiltration alone"],"ans":1,"expl":"Convection uses hydrostatic pressure to create solvent drag, pulling solutes along with fluid across the membrane. This is the primary mechanism in hemofiltration-based modalities like CVVH."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"In CVVHD (Continuous Veno-Venous Hemodialysis), what is the primary mechanism of solute clearance?","opts":["Convection with replacement fluid","Diffusion with dialysate flow","Adsorption to membrane","Plasma separation"],"ans":1,"expl":"CVVHD uses dialysate flowing counter-current to blood, creating concentration gradients that drive diffusive solute clearance, similar to conventional hemodialysis but at lower flow rates."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"What distinguishes CVVHDF from CVVH and CVVHD?","opts":["It uses only convection","It uses only diffusion","It combines both convection and diffusion","It uses no replacement fluid"],"ans":2,"expl":"CVVHDF (Continuous Veno-Venous Hemodiafiltration) combines both convective clearance (via replacement fluid) and diffusive clearance (via dialysate), maximizing solute removal across molecular weight ranges."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"SLED (Sustained Low-Efficiency Dialysis) is best described as:","opts":["A continuous 24-hour therapy","An extended intermittent therapy typically run 6-12 hours","A high-flow rapid dialysis session","A peritoneal dialysis variant"],"ans":1,"expl":"SLED is an extended intermittent renal replacement therapy, typically performed for 6-12 hours with lower blood and dialysate flows than conventional HD, offering hemodynamic stability between continuous and intermittent approaches."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"According to KDIGO guidelines, what is the recommended delivered CRRT dose for critically ill patients with AKI?","opts":["15-20 mL/kg/hr","20-25 mL/kg/hr","25-30 mL/kg/hr","35-40 mL/kg/hr"],"ans":1,"expl":"KDIGO recommends a delivered dose of 20-25 mL/kg/hr for CRRT in critically ill patients. Higher doses have not shown improved outcomes in major trials."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"Which vascular access site is preferred for CRRT catheter placement when feasible?","opts":["Femoral vein","Internal jugular vein","Subclavian vein","External jugular vein"],"ans":1,"expl":"The right internal jugular vein is preferred as it provides straight access to the SVC/RA junction, has lower infection risk than femoral, and avoids subclavian stenosis risk that could compromise future AV fistula creation."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"What is a key advantage of regional citrate anticoagulation over systemic heparin in CRRT?","opts":["Lower cost","Reduced bleeding risk","Simpler monitoring","Faster circuit clotting"],"ans":1,"expl":"Citrate anticoagulation works regionally within the circuit by chelating calcium, minimizing systemic anticoagulation effects and reducing bleeding risk, particularly beneficial in patients with coagulopathy or active bleeding."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"When using citrate anticoagulation in CRRT, which electrolyte requires close monitoring for potential accumulation?","opts":["Potassium","Sodium","Calcium","Phosphate"],"ans":2,"expl":"Citrate binds ionized calcium in the circuit. The citrate-calcium complex is metabolized by the liver, but in liver failure or severe shock, citrate can accumulate, leading to systemic hypocalcemia and metabolic alkalosis."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"In sepsis-induced AKI requiring CRRT, what is the primary indication for initiating renal replacement therapy?","opts":["Elevated creatinine alone","Life-threatening complications of AKI","Oliguria for 6 hours","BUN above 50 mg/dL"],"ans":1,"expl":"CRRT initiation in sepsis-induced AKI is based on life-threatening complications: severe acidosis, hyperkalemia, volume overload refractory to diuretics, or uremic complications—not solely on BUN/creatinine values."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"Which buffer solution is most commonly used in modern CRRT and provides better hemodynamic stability than lactate?","opts":["Acetate","Bicarbonate","Lactate","Citrate alone"],"ans":1,"expl":"Bicarbonate-buffered solutions are now standard in CRRT, providing more physiologic pH correction and better hemodynamic stability compared to lactate, which requires hepatic metabolism and can cause vasodilation."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"What is the primary cause of circuit clotting in CRRT?","opts":["Low blood flow rate","Inadequate anticoagulation","High ultrafiltration rate","All of the above"],"ans":3,"expl":"Circuit clotting can result from multiple factors: low blood flow causing stasis, inadequate anticoagulation allowing thrombus formation, and high ultrafiltration rates increasing hemoconcentration within the filter."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"During CRRT, transmembrane pressure (TMP) elevation most commonly indicates:","opts":["Filter clotting","Access recirculation","Hypotension","Electrolyte imbalance"],"ans":0,"expl":"Rising TMP suggests increased resistance to ultrafiltration, typically from filter membrane fouling or clotting. Progressive TMP elevation often precedes complete circuit thrombosis and signals need for circuit change."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"What is the typical blood flow rate range for CRRT?","opts":["50-100 mL/min","100-200 mL/min","200-300 mL/min","300-400 mL/min"],"ans":1,"expl":"CRRT typically uses blood flow rates of 100-200 mL/min, lower than conventional hemodialysis. This allows for gentler, continuous solute and fluid removal with better hemodynamic tolerance."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"Which complication is specifically associated with rapid correction of chronic hypernatremia during CRRT?","opts":["Cardiac arrhythmias","Cerebral edema","Hemolysis","Hypotension"],"ans":1,"expl":"Rapid correction of chronic hypernatremia can cause cerebral edema as brain cells, which have accumulated idiogenic osmoles, swell when extracellular tonicity decreases too quickly. Sodium correction should not exceed 10-12 mEq/L per 24 hours."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"In CRRT, what does the term 'effluent' refer to?","opts":["The blood returning to patient","The dialysate inflow","The waste fluid removed from circuit","The replacement fluid"],"ans":2,"expl":"Effluent is the total waste fluid removed from the CRRT circuit, including ultrafiltrate, spent dialysate, and any net fluid removal. Effluent flow rate determines the prescribed CRRT dose."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"Which electrolyte abnormality is most commonly encountered during initiation of CRRT in severe AKI?","opts":["Hypernatremia","Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypophosphatemia"],"ans":2,"expl":"Hyperkalemia is a common indication for urgent RRT initiation in AKI. CRRT effectively removes potassium, but initial rates should be monitored to avoid overly rapid shifts that could precipitate arrhythmias."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"What is the primary mechanism of phosphate removal in CRRT?","opts":["Diffusion only","Convection only","Both diffusion and convection","Adsorption to membrane"],"ans":2,"expl":"Phosphate is removed by both diffusion and convection in CRRT. However, phosphate clearance is often inadequate, leading to hypophosphatemia during prolonged CRRT, requiring supplementation."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"Which factor most significantly reduces delivered CRRT dose compared to prescribed dose?","opts":["Blood flow rate","Replacement fluid rate","Treatment downtime","Dialysate temperature"],"ans":2,"expl":"Treatment downtime from circuit clotting, patient transport, procedures, or machine alarms significantly reduces delivered dose. Studies show delivered dose is often 15-30% lower than prescribed due to interruptions."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"For a patient with severe metabolic acidosis (pH 7.15) and AKI, which CRRT prescription adjustment would best address the acidosis?","opts":["Decrease effluent rate","Increase bicarbonate concentration in dialysate/replacement fluid","Reduce blood flow rate","Switch to heparin anticoagulation"],"ans":1,"expl":"Increasing bicarbonate concentration in the dialysate or replacement fluid enhances bicarbonate delivery, helping correct metabolic acidosis. Standard solutions contain 25-32 mEq/L bicarbonate, which can be adjusted based on acid-base status."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","q":"What is the primary advantage of continuous renal replacement therapies over intermittent hemodialysis in hemodynamically unstable patients?","opts":["Lower cost","Faster solute clearance","Better hemodynamic tolerance","Shorter treatment time"],"ans":2,"expl":"CRRT provides slow, continuous fluid and solute removal over 24 hours, avoiding the rapid osmotic and volume shifts of intermittent HD that can precipitate hypotension in unstable patients."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A patient with stage 4 CKD has a hemoglobin level of 9.2 g/dL. What is the primary mechanism causing anemia in this patient?","opts":["Decreased erythropoietin production by the kidneys","Increased red blood cell destruction","Acute blood loss from the GI tract","Vitamin B12 deficiency"],"ans":0,"expl":"In CKD, damaged kidneys produce less erythropoietin (EPO), the hormone that stimulates red blood cell production in bone marrow. This is the primary cause of anemia in CKD patients."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which laboratory finding is most characteristic of secondary hyperparathyroidism in CKD?","opts":["Elevated PTH with low or normal calcium","Low PTH with high calcium","Normal PTH with low phosphate","Elevated PTH with high calcium"],"ans":0,"expl":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism in CKD presents with elevated PTH as the parathyroid glands overwork to compensate for low calcium (caused by low vitamin D activation) and high phosphate levels."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A CKD patient presents with metabolic acidosis. Which mechanism best explains this complication?","opts":["Decreased ability to excrete hydrogen ions and regenerate bicarbonate","Increased production of lactic acid","Excessive loss of bicarbonate through diarrhea","Respiratory compensation failure"],"ans":0,"expl":"Damaged kidneys lose the ability to excrete acid (hydrogen ions) and regenerate bicarbonate, leading to metabolic acidosis with a normal anion gap."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"What is the primary nursing concern when a CKD patient has a serum potassium level of 6.2 mEq/L?","opts":["Risk of life-threatening cardiac arrhythmias","Increased risk of bleeding","Development of metabolic alkalosis","Progression of bone disease"],"ans":0,"expl":"Hyperkalemia above 6.0 mEq/L can cause dangerous cardiac arrhythmias including ventricular fibrillation. This is a medical emergency requiring immediate intervention."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which intervention is most appropriate for managing hyperphosphatemia in a CKD patient?","opts":["Administering phosphate binders with meals","Increasing dietary phosphate intake","Giving vitamin D supplements alone","Restricting fluid intake"],"ans":0,"expl":"Phosphate binders should be taken with meals to bind dietary phosphate in the gut, preventing its absorption. This is a cornerstone of CKD-MBD management."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A patient with diabetic nephropathy is at highest risk for which cardiovascular complication?","opts":["Accelerated atherosclerosis and coronary artery disease","Pulmonary hypertension","Aortic stenosis","Peripheral varicose veins"],"ans":0,"expl":"Diabetic nephropathy significantly increases cardiovascular risk due to accelerated atherosclerosis, endothelial dysfunction, and chronic inflammation associated with both diabetes and CKD."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"What is the target hemoglobin range for most CKD patients being treated with erythropoiesis-stimulating agents (ESAs)?","opts":["10-11 g/dL","12-14 g/dL","8-9 g/dL","14-16 g/dL"],"ans":0,"expl":"Current guidelines recommend targeting hemoglobin of 10-11 g/dL in CKD patients on ESAs. Higher targets increase cardiovascular risk without improving quality of life."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which type of bone disease is characterized by low bone turnover and suppressed PTH in CKD patients?","opts":["Adynamic bone disease","Osteitis fibrosa cystica","Osteoporosis","Paget's disease"],"ans":0,"expl":"Adynamic bone disease features low bone turnover with suppressed PTH, often from over-suppression with vitamin D analogs or calcium-based binders. It increases fracture and calcification risk."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"What is the primary reason CKD patients develop dyslipidemia?","opts":["Decreased clearance of triglyceride-rich lipoproteins","Increased dietary fat absorption","Enhanced HDL production","Reduced cholesterol synthesis"],"ans":0,"expl":"CKD causes dyslipidemia primarily through decreased clearance of triglyceride-rich lipoproteins (VLDL, chylomicrons) due to reduced lipoprotein lipase activity."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A CKD patient complains of persistent nausea, loss of appetite, and muscle wasting. Which complication should the nurse suspect?","opts":["Protein-energy wasting (malnutrition)","Hypercalcemia","Metabolic alkalosis","Iron overload"],"ans":0,"expl":"These symptoms indicate protein-energy wasting, a common complication in advanced CKD caused by uremic toxins, inflammation, metabolic acidosis, and inadequate nutrient intake."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which form of vitamin D requires activation by the kidneys and is typically deficient in CKD patients?","opts":["Calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D)","Cholecalciferol (vitamin D3)","Ergocalciferol (vitamin D2)","25-hydroxyvitamin D"],"ans":0,"expl":"Calcitriol is the active form of vitamin D produced by kidney 1-alpha-hydroxylase. In CKD, this enzyme activity decreases, leading to calcitriol deficiency and impaired calcium absorption."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"What is the most common cause of death in patients with chronic kidney disease?","opts":["Cardiovascular disease","Infection","Progression to ESRD","Malignancy"],"ans":0,"expl":"Cardiovascular disease accounts for approximately 50% of deaths in CKD patients, with risk increasing as kidney function declines. This includes coronary artery disease, heart failure, and sudden cardiac death."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A patient with CKD has elevated intact PTH, elevated phosphate, and low calcium. What stage of CKD-MBD does this represent?","opts":["Secondary hyperparathyroidism","Primary hyperparathyroidism","Tertiary hyperparathyroidism","Hypoparathyroidism"],"ans":0,"expl":"This pattern indicates secondary hyperparathyroidism: the parathyroid glands increase PTH production in response to low calcium and high phosphate from impaired kidney function."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which dietary modification is most important for a CKD patient with hyperkalemia?","opts":["Limiting high-potassium foods like bananas, oranges, and potatoes","Increasing protein intake","Restricting all dairy products","Eliminating all carbohydrates"],"ans":0,"expl":"Patients with hyperkalemia should limit high-potassium foods. Common sources include bananas, oranges, tomatoes, potatoes, spinach, and salt substitutes containing potassium chloride."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"What is the role of sevelamer in CKD management?","opts":["Non-calcium phosphate binder that reduces phosphate absorption","Potassium-lowering medication","Erythropoietin stimulator","Vitamin D analog"],"ans":0,"expl":"Sevelamer is a non-calcium, non-aluminum phosphate binder that binds dietary phosphate in the intestine. It's preferred when calcium levels are elevated or vascular calcification is present."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which finding suggests uremic pruritus in a dialysis patient?","opts":["Generalized itching without visible rash, worse at night","Localized rash with vesicles","Hives appearing after medication administration","Dry, scaly patches only on extremities"],"ans":0,"expl":"Uremic pruritus presents as generalized itching without primary skin lesions, often worse at night. It's related to uremic toxins, dry skin, and immune dysregulation in CKD."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"A CKD patient is prescribed sodium bicarbonate. What complication is being treated?","opts":["Metabolic acidosis","Hyperkalemia","Hyperphosphatemia","Anemia"],"ans":0,"expl":"Sodium bicarbonate treats metabolic acidosis by providing bicarbonate to buffer excess acid. Target serum bicarbonate is typically 22-26 mEq/L in CKD patients."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"What is the primary mechanism by which CKD increases fracture risk?","opts":["CKD-mineral and bone disorder (CKD-MBD) affecting bone quality","Increased physical activity","Excessive vitamin D intake","Low phosphate levels"],"ans":0,"expl":"CKD-MBD causes abnormal bone turnover, mineralization, and strength through disturbances in calcium, phosphate, PTH, and vitamin D metabolism, significantly increasing fracture risk."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"Which iron study pattern is typical in CKD-related anemia?","opts":["Normal or high ferritin with low transferrin saturation","Low ferritin with high transferrin saturation","Low ferritin with low transferrin saturation","High ferritin with high transferrin saturation"],"ans":0,"expl":"CKD anemia often shows functional iron deficiency: normal/high ferritin (acute phase reactant) but low transferrin saturation due to inflammation blocking iron release from stores."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","q":"What is the significance of elevated FGF23 in early CKD?","opts":["Early marker of phosphate retention and predictor of cardiovascular events","Indicator of improved kidney function","Sign of iron deficiency","Marker of resolved metabolic acidosis"],"ans":0,"expl":"FGF23 rises early in CKD to promote phosphate excretion. However, elevated FGF23 is independently associated with left ventricular hypertrophy and increased cardiovascular mortality."}];
const ORIG_FLASHCARDS = [{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)","back":"Sudden decline in kidney function over hours to days, characterized by increased creatinine and/or decreased urine output."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)","back":"Progressive, irreversible loss of kidney function over 3+ months, classified into 5 stages based on GFR."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Prerenal AKI","back":"Kidney dysfunction caused by decreased blood flow to the kidneys before the renal tissue itself is damaged."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Intrarenal AKI","back":"Kidney damage occurring within the kidney tissue itself, often from toxins, ischemia, or inflammation."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Postrenal AKI","back":"Kidney dysfunction caused by obstruction of urine flow after it leaves the kidneys."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN)","back":"Death of tubular epithelial cells, usually from ischemia or nephrotoxins, causing intrinsic AKI."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR)","back":"Volume of fluid filtered by glomeruli per minute; best measure of overall kidney function."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Nephrotic Syndrome","back":"Clinical syndrome with heavy proteinuria (>3.5g/day), hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Nephritic Syndrome","back":"Clinical syndrome with hematuria, RBC casts, hypertension, and mild-to-moderate proteinuria."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"IgA Nephropathy","back":"Most common glomerulonephritis; immune complex deposition in glomeruli causing hematuria and proteinuria."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Hyperkalemia","back":"Elevated serum potassium (>5.0 mEq/L); dangerous in CKD due to risk of cardiac arrhythmias."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Uremia","back":"Clinical syndrome of kidney failure with accumulation of nitrogenous wastes causing nausea, confusion, and pericarditis."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Erythropoietin","back":"Hormone produced by kidneys that stimulates red blood cell production; deficient in CKD."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Metabolic Acidosis in CKD","back":"Low blood pH from impaired hydrogen ion excretion and bicarbonate regeneration by damaged kidneys."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Renal Replacement Therapy","back":"Treatments that replace kidney function: hemodialysis, peritoneal dialysis, or kidney transplantation."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Muddy Brown Casts","back":"Granular casts in urine sediment pathognomonic for acute tubular necrosis."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Oliguria","back":"Reduced urine output (<400 mL/day in adults); common sign of AKI."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Hyperphosphatemia","back":"Elevated serum phosphate; common in CKD due to decreased excretion, leading to bone disease."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"Diabetic Nephropathy","back":"Kidney damage from diabetes; leading cause of CKD and end-stage renal disease worldwide."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","front":"K/DOQI Guidelines","back":"Clinical practice guidelines for CKD evaluation, classification, and management by National Kidney Foundation."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Diffusion","back":"Movement of solutes from high to low concentration across a semipermeable membrane; primary clearance mechanism in HD"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Convection","back":"Solute movement with water flow across membrane (solvent drag); important for middle molecule clearance"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Ultrafiltration","back":"Removal of excess fluid by applying hydrostatic pressure across the dialyzer membrane"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Kt/V","back":"Measure of dialysis adequacy: K=clearance, t=time, V=volume of urea distribution; minimum 1.2 recommended"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Arteriovenous Fistula (AVF)","back":"Surgical connection of artery to vein; gold standard access with lowest infection risk and best patency"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Arteriovenous Graft (AVG)","back":"Synthetic tube connecting artery to vein; matures faster than AVF but higher infection and thrombosis risk"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Dialyzer","back":"Artificial kidney containing semipermeable membrane where blood and dialysate flow in opposite directions"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Dialysate","back":"Specially formulated solution containing electrolytes and bicarbonate that flows counter-current to blood in dialyzer"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Heparin","back":"Anticoagulant used during HD to prevent clotting in extracorporeal circuit"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Intradialytic Hypotension","back":"Most common HD complication; drop in BP during treatment due to rapid fluid removal or autonomic dysfunction"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome","back":"Neurological complication from rapid solute removal causing cerebral edema; presents with headache, confusion, seizures"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Air Embolism","back":"Life-threatening complication when air enters circulation; symptoms include chest pain, dyspnea; treat with left lateral decubitus position"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"High-Flux Dialyzer","back":"Dialyzer with larger pores allowing better middle molecule clearance (e.g., beta-2 microglobulin)"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Thrill","back":"Vibration felt over functioning AV access; absence suggests thrombosis or stenosis"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Bruit","back":"Whooshing sound heard over AV access with stethoscope; indicates blood flow through access"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Steal Syndrome","back":"Complication where access diverts blood flow away from hand, causing ischemia, pain, and coolness"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Dialysate Sodium","back":"Typically 135-145 mEq/L; matches serum levels to prevent osmotic shifts and cramping"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Cool Dialysate","back":"Temperature 35-36°C; promotes vasoconstriction and reduces intradialytic hypotension"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Beta-2 Microglobulin","back":"Middle molecule toxin cleared better by high-flux dialyzers; accumulates in ESRD causing amyloidosis"},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","front":"Extracorporeal Circuit","back":"Blood pathway outside body during HD: access → blood tubing → dialyzer → return to patient"},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Tenckhoff catheter","back":"Most common PD catheter type; silicone tube with one or two cuffs for tissue ingrowth and infection barrier."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Exit-site infection","back":"Infection at catheter skin exit characterized by erythema, tenderness, and/or purulent drainage."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Peritonitis (PD)","back":"Inflammation of peritoneal membrane; diagnosed by cloudy effluent, abdominal pain, and/or WBC >100/μL in effluent."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Peritoneal Equilibration Test (PET)","back":"4-hour test measuring solute transport rate; classifies patients as high/low transporters to guide prescription."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"High transporter","back":"Patient with rapid solute equilibration; loses osmotic gradient quickly, needs shorter dwells or icodextrin."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Low transporter","back":"Patient with slow solute equilibration; benefits from longer dwells for adequate clearance."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"CAPD","back":"Continuous Ambulatory Peritoneal Dialysis; manual exchanges 4-5 times daily without machine."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"CCPD","back":"Continuous Cyclic Peritoneal Dialysis; machine-assisted overnight exchanges with daytime dwell."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Ultrafiltration","back":"Fluid removal across peritoneal membrane driven by osmotic gradient from glucose or icodextrin."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Ultrafiltration failure","back":"Inability to achieve adequate fluid removal despite hypertonic exchanges; may indicate membrane changes."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Icodextrin","back":"Glucose polymer osmotic agent for long dwells; maintains UF in high transporters and overnight."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Dwell time","back":"Duration dialysate remains in peritoneal cavity; affects solute clearance and ultrafiltration volume."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Dialysate","back":"Sterile solution containing electrolytes, buffer (lactate/bicarbonate), and osmotic agent (glucose/icodextrin)."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Peritoneal membrane","back":"Semipermeable membrane lining abdominal cavity; acts as dialysis barrier between blood and dialysate."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Catheter cuff","back":"Dacron or polyester material promoting tissue ingrowth to secure catheter and prevent infection migration."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Break-in period","back":"2-4 weeks post-catheter placement before full-volume PD; allows healing and reduces leak risk."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Residual renal function","back":"Remaining native kidney function; contributes significantly to total clearance in early PD patients."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Peritoneal dialysis adequacy","back":"Measured by Kt/V (target ≥1.7/week) and creatinine clearance (target ≥50 L/week/1.73m²)."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Encapsulating Peritoneal Sclerosis","back":"Rare severe complication with fibrous bowel encapsulation causing obstruction; requires surgery."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","front":"Empiric peritonitis therapy","back":"Initial antibiotics covering Gram-positive (vancomycin/cephalosporin) AND Gram-negative (ceftazidime/aminoglycoside)."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"Hyperacute Rejection","back":"Rejection occurring within minutes to hours due to pre-formed antibodies against donor antigens; causes immediate graft thrombosis and is prevented by proper crossmatch testing."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"Delayed Graft Function (DGF)","back":"Need for dialysis within the first week post-transplant; more common with deceased donors, prolonged cold ischemia time, and associated with increased acute rejection risk."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"Calcineurin Inhibitor Nephrotoxicity","back":"Dose-dependent kidney injury from tacrolimus or cyclosporine causing afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction; managed by therapeutic drug monitoring and dose adjustment."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"CMV D+/R- Risk","back":"Highest risk CMV scenario: seronegative recipient receives organ from seropositive donor; requires antiviral prophylaxis (valganciclovir) for 3-6 months minimum."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"BK Virus Nephropathy","back":"Polyomavirus infection of renal tubular cells causing interstitial nephritis and potential graft loss; screened via urine decoy cells or plasma viral load; treated by reducing immunosuppression."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"Induction Therapy","back":"Intense immunosuppression given at transplant (basiliximab or ATG) to prevent early acute rejection while allowing delayed introduction of nephrotoxic maintenance agents."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"Triple Immunosuppression","back":"Standard maintenance regimen: calcineurin inhibitor (tacrolimus) + antimetabolite (mycophenolate) + corticosteroid; provides synergistic effect with reduced individual drug toxicity."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"Transplant Glomerulopathy","back":"Hallmark of chronic antibody-mediated rejection; characterized by glomerular basement membrane double contours on biopsy, often with C4d deposition."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"Panel Reactive Antibody (PRA)","back":"Percentage of HLA antigens against which a patient has pre-formed antibodies; high PRA (>80%) indicates sensitization and makes finding compatible donors more difficult."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"Cold Ischemia Time","back":"Duration kidney is preserved on ice between procurement and implantation; longer times (>24 hours) increase risk of DGF and may impact long-term graft survival."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"Donor-Specific Antibody (DSA)","back":"HLA antibodies in recipient directed against donor antigens; detected by Luminex assay; associated with antibody-mediated rejection and poorer graft outcomes."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"PJP Prophylaxis","back":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole given for 6-12 months post-transplant to prevent Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia; also protects against Toxoplasma and some bacterial infections."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"Post-Transplant Diabetes Mellitus (PTDM)","back":"New-onset diabetes after transplantation; risk factors include tacrolimus, corticosteroids, obesity, and family history; requires glucose monitoring and may need insulin or oral agents."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"Acute Cellular Rejection","back":"T-cell mediated rejection typically occurring in first 3-6 months; diagnosed by biopsy showing tubulitis and interstitial inflammation; treated with pulse steroids."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"Living Donor Advantage","back":"Better outcomes than deceased donor due to HLA matching potential, shorter cold ischemia time, elective scheduling, and ability to transplant before dialysis dependence."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"mTOR Inhibitors","back":"Sirolimus and everolimus block IL-2 signaling pathway; used as alternative to calcineurin inhibitors; side effects include impaired wound healing, proteinuria, and hyperlipidemia."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"Crossmatch Test","back":"Pre-transplant test mixing recipient serum with donor lymphocytes; positive result indicates pre-formed antibodies and contraindicates transplantation due to hyperacute rejection risk."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"Death with Functioning Graft","back":"Leading cause of graft loss in first year; primarily from cardiovascular disease and infection; reflects success of rejection prevention but challenges with immunosuppression complications."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"University of Wisconsin Solution","back":"Standard preservation fluid for deceased donor kidneys; contains impermeants and substrates that minimize cellular injury during cold storage; allows 24-48 hour preservation."},{"topic":"Transplant","front":"Chronic Rejection","back":"Slow graft deterioration over months to years; characterized by interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy, and/or transplant glomerulopathy; generally irreversible and leads to graft failure."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"Convection (CRRT)","back":"Solute removal mechanism using hydrostatic pressure to create solvent drag across membrane; primary in CVVH"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"Diffusion (CRRT)","back":"Solute removal via concentration gradient between blood and dialysate; primary mechanism in CVVHD"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"CVVH","back":"Continuous Veno-Venous Hemofiltration: uses convection with replacement fluid for solute clearance"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"CVVHD","back":"Continuous Veno-Venous Hemodialysis: uses diffusion with dialysate flow for solute clearance"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"CVVHDF","back":"Continuous Veno-Venous Hemodiafiltration: combines convection and diffusion for maximal clearance"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"SLED","back":"Sustained Low-Efficiency Dialysis: extended intermittent RRT (6-12 hrs) with lower flows than conventional HD"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"KDIGO CRRT Dose","back":"Recommended delivered dose: 20-25 mL/kg/hr for critically ill AKI patients"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"Citrate Anticoagulation","back":"Regional anticoagulation via calcium chelation in circuit; reduces bleeding risk vs systemic heparin"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"Citrate Toxicity Signs","back":"Hypocalcemia, metabolic alkalosis, elevated total-to-ionized calcium ratio (>2.5); seen in liver failure"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"Preferred CRRT Access","back":"Right internal jugular vein: straight path, lower infection risk, preserves subclavian for future fistula"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"Transmembrane Pressure (TMP)","back":"Pressure gradient across filter membrane; rising TMP indicates filter clotting or fouling"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"Effluent Flow Rate","back":"Total waste fluid rate (ultrafiltrate + dialysate + net removal); determines prescribed CRRT dose"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"CRRT Blood Flow Rate","back":"Typical range: 100-200 mL/min, lower than conventional HD for hemodynamic stability"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"Hyperkalemia Management (CRRT)","back":"CRRT indication in AKI; monitor for rapid shifts during initiation to prevent arrhythmias"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"CRRT-Associated Hypophosphatemia","back":"Common during prolonged CRRT due to inadequate phosphate clearance; requires supplementation"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"Bicarbonate Buffer (CRRT)","back":"Standard modern CRRT buffer; more physiologic than lactate, better hemodynamic stability"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"CRRT Downtime Impact","back":"Circuit clotting, transport, procedures reduce delivered dose 15-30% below prescribed"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"Hyponatremia Correction Limit","back":"Do not increase serum Na+ >10-12 mEq/L per 24 hours to avoid osmotic demyelination"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"Hypernatremia Correction Limit","back":"Do not decrease serum Na+ >10-12 mEq/L per 24 hours to avoid cerebral edema"},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","front":"Sepsis-Induced AKI RRT Indication","back":"Life-threatening complications: severe acidosis, hyperkalemia, refractory volume overload, uremia"},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Erythropoietin (EPO) deficiency in CKD","back":"Damaged kidneys produce less EPO, causing normocytic normochromic anemia. Treated with ESAs and iron supplementation."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"CKD-MBD (Chronic Kidney Disease-Mineral and Bone Disorder)","back":"Systemic disorder of mineral metabolism (calcium, phosphate, PTH, vitamin D) causing bone abnormalities and vascular calcification."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Secondary Hyperparathyroidism","back":"Elevated PTH due to kidney's inability to activate vitamin D and excrete phosphate, causing low calcium that stimulates parathyroid glands."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Adynamic Bone Disease","back":"Low bone turnover state with suppressed PTH (<150 pg/mL). Risk factors: overuse of vitamin D analogs, calcium-based binders, diabetes."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Metabolic Acidosis in CKD","back":"Normal anion gap acidosis from decreased acid excretion and bicarbonate regeneration. Accelerates CKD progression and causes bone buffering."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Hyperkalemia threshold requiring intervention","back":"Potassium >5.5 mEq/L requires dietary modification; >6.0 mEq/L is dangerous and may need emergency treatment (calcium, insulin/glucose, dialysis)."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Phosphate binder timing","back":"Must be taken WITH meals to bind dietary phosphate in the gut. Taking between meals is ineffective."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Calcimimetics (e.g., cinacalcet)","back":"Medications that increase calcium-sensing receptor sensitivity, lowering PTH, calcium, and phosphate. Used in secondary hyperparathyroidism."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Vitamin D activation pathway","back":"Skin/supplements (D3/D2) → Liver (25-OH-D) → Kidney 1-alpha-hydroxylase (1,25-OH-D/calcitriol). Kidney step impaired in CKD."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Protein-energy wasting (PEW)","back":"Loss of protein and energy reserves in CKD. Causes: uremic toxins, inflammation, acidosis, anorexia. Increases mortality risk."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Dyslipidemia pattern in CKD","back":"High triglycerides, low HDL, normal or mildly elevated LDL. Caused by decreased lipoprotein lipase activity and impaired clearance."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Cardiovascular risk in CKD","back":"CKD is an independent CVD risk factor. Mechanisms: hypertension, anemia, vascular calcification, inflammation, endothelial dysfunction."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Uremic pruritus","back":"Generalized itching in advanced CKD/dialysis. Related to uremic toxins, xerosis, immune activation. Treat with emollients, gabapentin, optimized dialysis."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Target hemoglobin with ESA therapy","back":"10-11 g/dL. Avoid >11.5 g/dL due to increased stroke, thrombosis, and mortality risk without QOL benefit."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Functional iron deficiency","back":"Adequate iron stores (normal/high ferritin) but unavailable for erythropoiesis (low TSAT). Common in CKD due to inflammation/hepcidin."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Vascular calcification in CKD","back":"Calcium-phosphate deposition in arterial media. Accelerated by hyperphosphatemia, high calcium load, vitamin D analogs. Increases CV mortality."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Diabetic nephropathy progression","back":"Hyperglycemia → glomerular hyperfiltration → microalbuminuria → macroalbuminuria → declining GFR → ESRD. ACEi/ARB slow progression."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Sodium bicarbonate therapy indication","back":"Serum bicarbonate <22 mEq/L. Goal: 22-26 mEq/L. Slows CKD progression, improves bone health, reduces protein catabolism."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"FGF23 in CKD","back":"Fibroblast growth factor 23 rises early to increase phosphate excretion. High levels predict LVH, cardiovascular events, and mortality."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","front":"Osteitis fibrosa cystica","back":"High bone turnover disease from severe secondary hyperparathyroidism. Features: elevated PTH, bone pain, fractures, brown tumors."}];
const ORIG_LESSONS = [{"topic":"Kidney Disease","title":"Understanding Acute Kidney Injury: Types and Causes","body":"Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) is classified into three categories based on the location of the problem. Prerenal AKI occurs when blood flow to the kidneys is reduced before reaching the renal tissue. Common causes include hypotension, heart failure, sepsis, dehydration, and hemorrhage. The kidney tissue itself is initially healthy but cannot filter properly without adequate perfusion. Intrarenal (intrinsic) AKI involves direct damage to the kidney tissue itself. The most common form is acute tubular necrosis (ATN), caused by prolonged ischemia or nephrotoxic substances like contrast dye, aminoglycoside antibiotics, or NSAIDs. Other intrarenal causes include glomerulonephritis, interstitial nephritis, and vascular diseases. Postrenal AKI results from obstruction of urine flow after it leaves the kidneys. Causes include kidney stones, bladder tumors, prostate enlargement, ureteral strictures, or blood clots. Early recognition of the type is critical because treatment differs: prerenal requires volume resuscitation and improving perfusion, intrarenal needs removal of offending agents and supportive care, and postrenal requires relieving the obstruction. Nurses should monitor urine output, serum creatinine trends, and urine sediment findings to help identify the type and guide interventions."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","title":"Chronic Kidney Disease: Staging and Management","body":"Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) is classified into five stages based on glomerular filtration rate (GFR). Stage 1 (GFR ≥90) has normal or high GFR with kidney damage markers like proteinuria. Stage 2 (GFR 60-89) shows mild reduction. Stage 3 (GFR 30-59) is moderate reduction, often when symptoms begin appearing. Stage 4 (GFR 15-29) is severe reduction requiring preparation for renal replacement therapy. Stage 5 (GFR <15) is kidney failure requiring dialysis or transplantation. Key management principles include blood pressure control (target <130/80 mmHg), preferably with ACE inhibitors or ARBs which also reduce proteinuria. Glycemic control is essential for diabetic patients. Dietary modifications include sodium restriction (<2g/day), protein moderation (0.6-0.8g/kg/day in stages 3-5), potassium restriction if hyperkalemic, and phosphate restriction with binders if hyperphosphatemic. Anemia management involves erythropoiesis-stimulating agents and iron supplementation. Bone-mineral disorder is managed with vitamin D analogs, phosphate binders, and calcimimetics. Regular monitoring includes GFR, electrolytes, hemoglobin, calcium, phosphorus, PTH, and urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio. Patient education focuses on medication adherence, dietary compliance, recognizing complications, and understanding treatment options including dialysis modalities and transplantation."},{"topic":"Kidney Disease","title":"Glomerular Diseases: Nephrotic vs Nephritic Syndromes","body":"Glomerular diseases present as either nephrotic or nephritic syndromes, distinguished by their clinical features and underlying pathology. Nephrotic syndrome results from increased glomerular permeability to proteins. Classic findings include heavy proteinuria (>3.5g/day), hypoalbuminemia (<3.0g/dL), generalized edema (often periorbital and dependent), hyperlipidemia, and lipiduria. Common causes include minimal change disease (common in children), focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, membranous nephropathy, and diabetic nephropathy. Complications include increased infection risk (loss of immunoglobulins), thromboembolism (loss of antithrombin III), and accelerated atherosclerosis. Treatment involves corticosteroids or immunosuppressants for primary causes, ACE inhibitors/ARBs to reduce proteinuria, diuretics for edema, statins for hyperlipidemia, and anticoagulation if albumin is very low. Nephritic syndrome involves glomerular inflammation with capillary wall damage. Hallmark findings include hematuria with dysmorphic RBCs and RBC casts, hypertension, oliguria, mild-to-moderate proteinuria (<3.5g/day), and azotemia. Causes include IgA nephropathy (most common worldwide), post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, lupus nephritis, and rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis. Management focuses on treating the underlying cause, blood pressure control, sodium and fluid restriction for edema, and immunosuppression for autoimmune causes. Urinalysis showing RBC casts is pathognomonic for glomerulonephritis and distinguishes nephritic from nephrotic presentation. Both syndromes require nephrology consultation and often kidney biopsy for definitive diagnosis."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","title":"Principles of Hemodialysis: Diffusion, Convection, and Ultrafiltration","body":"Hemodialysis relies on three key principles to remove waste and excess fluid from the blood. DIFFUSION is the primary mechanism, where solutes move from areas of high concentration (blood) to low concentration (dialysate) across a semipermeable membrane. Small molecules like urea, creatinine, and potassium are efficiently cleared this way. CONVECTION occurs when solutes are dragged along with water as it moves across the membrane due to pressure gradients, particularly important for clearing middle-sized molecules. ULTRAFILTRATION is the controlled removal of excess fluid by applying hydrostatic pressure, forcing plasma water through the membrane pores. The dialyzer membrane's characteristics determine what can pass through: pore size controls the molecular weight cutoff, typically allowing molecules under 50,000 Daltons while retaining albumin and blood cells. High-flux membranes have larger pores for better middle molecule clearance. Blood and dialysate flow in opposite directions (counter-current) to maximize the concentration gradient throughout the dialyzer length."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","title":"Vascular Access for Hemodialysis: Types, Care, and Complications","body":"Vascular access is the lifeline for hemodialysis patients. There are three main types: ARTERIOVENOUS FISTULA (AVF), ARTERIOVENOUS GRAFT (AVG), and CENTRAL VENOUS CATHETER. AVF is created by surgically connecting an artery to a vein, usually in the arm. It requires 3-6 months to mature but offers the best long-term patency and lowest infection risk, making it the gold standard. A functioning AVF has a palpable thrill and audible bruit. AVG uses a synthetic tube to connect artery and vein, maturing in 2-4 weeks but with higher thrombosis and infection rates. Catheters (tunneled or non-tunneled) provide immediate access but carry the highest infection and thrombosis risk, reserved for temporary use. COMPLICATIONS include: thrombosis (loss of thrill/bruit), stenosis (reduced flow, prolonged bleeding after needle removal), infection (redness, warmth, fever), aneurysm formation (repeated cannulation sites), and steal syndrome (hand ischemia from diverted blood flow). Proper care includes daily inspection, avoiding blood pressure measurements or IVs in the access arm, and immediate reporting of any changes in thrill or bruit."},{"topic":"Hemodialysis","title":"Intradialytic Complications: Recognition and Management","body":"Several complications can occur during hemodialysis treatments. INTRADIALYTIC HYPOTENSION is the most common, affecting 20-30% of treatments. Causes include rapid ultrafiltration, autonomic dysfunction, and cardiac issues. Management includes stopping ultrafiltration, placing patient in Trendelenburg position, administering saline bolus, and using cool dialysate for prevention. MUSCLE CRAMPS occur in 5-20% of treatments, typically from rapid volume depletion. Treatment includes stretching, hypertonic saline, or reducing ultrafiltration rate. DIALYSIS DISEQUILIBRIUM SYNDROME results from rapid solute removal causing cerebral edema, presenting with headache, nausea, confusion, and potentially seizures. Prevention involves slower, more frequent treatments for new patients. AIR EMBOLISM is rare but life-threatening, occurring when air enters the venous line. Symptoms include sudden chest pain, dyspnea, cough, and cardiovascular collapse. Immediate management: clamp venous line, place patient in left lateral decubitus with head down (Durant's maneuver), administer 100% oxygen, and call for emergency support. Other complications include hemolysis (from overheated dialysate or chloramine exposure), allergic reactions to dialyzer membrane, and bleeding from excessive anticoagulation."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","title":"PD Catheter Types and Placement","body":"Peritoneal dialysis requires secure vascular access via a PD catheter. The Tenckhoff catheter is the most widely used design, featuring a silicone tube with one or two cuffs made of Dacron or polyester. The cuffs promote tissue ingrowth, anchoring the catheter and creating a barrier against bacterial migration. Catheters can be placed surgically (open or laparoscopic), percutaneously, or with fluoroscopic guidance. Laparoscopic placement allows direct visualization, omentopexy if needed, and concurrent hernia repair. Catheter types include standard Tenckhoff (coiled or straight tip), presternal catheters (exit site on chest wall for patients with abdominal ostomies or severe obesity), and moncrief-popovich catheters (external segment buried until use). Proper catheter positioning places the tip in the pelvis (pouch of Douglas) to ensure complete drainage. Break-in period of 2-4 weeks with low-volume exchanges allows healing and reduces pericatheter leak risk. Exit-site should be oriented downward or laterally to minimize trauma and contamination risk."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","title":"Peritoneal Membrane Transport and the PET","body":"The Peritoneal Equilibration Test (PET) characterizes individual membrane transport properties to optimize PD prescriptions. During PET, a standard 2.5% dextrose exchange is performed with dialysate and blood samples collected at specified intervals. The dialysate-to-plasma (D/P) creatinine ratio at 4 hours determines transporter status: high (>0.81), high-average (0.65-0.81), low-average (0.49-0.64), or low (<0.49). High transporters rapidly equilibrate solutes but quickly lose the osmotic gradient, resulting in poor ultrafiltration on long dwells. They benefit from shorter dwell times, automated PD (APD), or icodextrin for long dwells. Low transporters have slower solute clearance but maintain UF longer; they need extended dwell times and may require higher glucose concentrations. Understanding transporter status guides modality selection (CAPD vs. APD), dwell times, and dialysate composition. Repeat PET annually or when clinical changes occur (UF failure, inadequate clearance)."},{"topic":"Peritoneal Dialysis","title":"Peritonitis Recognition and Management","body":"Peritonitis is the most serious common complication of PD, requiring prompt recognition and treatment. Diagnostic criteria include at least two of: cloudy effluent, abdominal pain, and/or effluent WBC >100/μL with >50% neutrophils. Gram stain and culture should be obtained before antibiotics. Empiric therapy must cover both Gram-positive organisms (vancomycin 15-30 mg/kg IP or cephalosporin) and Gram-negative organisms (ceftazidime 1g IP or aminoglycoside). Intraperitoneal administration achieves superior peritoneal tissue levels compared to IV. Treatment continues for 2-3 weeks depending on organism. Catheter removal is indicated for refractory peritonitis (no improvement after 5 days), fungal peritonitis, relapsing peritonitis, or severe exit-site/tunnel infection. Prevention focuses on proper exchange technique, exit-site care with topical mupirocin or gentamicin, and patient education. Recurrent peritonitis may indicate biofilm formation, requiring catheter removal. Early recognition and appropriate antibiotic selection preserve membrane function and prevent complications like encapsulating peritoneal sclerosis."},{"topic":"Transplant","title":"Types of Kidney Transplant Rejection","body":"Kidney transplant rejection is classified by timing and mechanism. Hyperacute rejection occurs within minutes to hours due to pre-existing antibodies against donor HLA antigens, causing immediate thrombosis and graft loss. This is now rare due to mandatory crossmatch testing. Acute rejection typically occurs within the first 3-6 months and has two forms: acute cellular rejection (T-cell mediated, showing tubulitis on biopsy) and acute antibody-mediated rejection (donor-specific antibodies causing complement activation). Both are treatable with intensified immunosuppression if caught early. Chronic rejection develops over months to years and is characterized by progressive interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy, and transplant glomerulopathy. Chronic antibody-mediated rejection shows glomerular basement membrane double contours and C4d deposition. Unlike acute rejection, chronic changes are largely irreversible and represent the leading cause of late graft loss. Prevention through consistent immunosuppression adherence and regular monitoring is critical."},{"topic":"Transplant","title":"Immunosuppressive Medication Management","body":"Kidney transplant recipients require lifelong immunosuppression to prevent rejection. Standard maintenance therapy uses triple drug regimens: a calcineurin inhibitor (tacrolimus preferred over cyclosporine), an antimetabolite (mycophenolate mofetil or mycophenolic acid), and corticosteroids. Tacrolimus requires therapeutic drug monitoring with trough levels typically targeted at 8-12 ng/mL in early post-transplant period and 5-8 ng/mL long-term, though targets vary by center and patient risk. Mycophenolate inhibits lymphocyte proliferation through IMPDH inhibition and does not require routine level monitoring. Corticosteroids are often tapered to low doses or discontinued in select patients but provide important anti-inflammatory effects. Induction therapy with basiliximab (IL-2 receptor antagonist) or antithymocyte globulin (T-cell depleting antibody) is given at transplant to provide intense early immunosuppression while allowing delayed introduction of nephrotoxic calcineurin inhibitors. Key nursing considerations include medication timing consistency, recognition of toxicity signs (tremor, hyperglycemia, nephrotoxicity), drug interaction awareness (CYP3A4 inhibitors/inducers affect tacrolimus levels), and patient education on adherence importance."},{"topic":"Transplant","title":"Infectious Complications Post-Transplant","body":"Infection is a leading cause of morbidity and mortality post-kidney transplant, with risk varying by time since transplant. In the first month, bacterial infections predominate (wound infections, UTI, pneumonia, line infections). Between 1-6 months, opportunistic infections emerge due to peak immunosuppression: CMV (especially D+/R- recipients), BK virus nephropathy, Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, and fungal infections. After 6 months, community-acquired infections become more common, though late opportunistic infections occur with intensified immunosuppression for rejection. CMV prophylaxis with valganciclovir is standard for 3-6 months in high-risk patients. PJP prophylaxis with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is given for 6-12 months. BK virus screening via plasma viral load or urine decoy cells is performed monthly for 3-6 months, then quarterly through year one. Elevated BK viral load prompts immunosuppression reduction as the primary treatment. All transplant recipients should receive appropriate vaccinations pre-transplant when possible (avoiding live vaccines post-transplant) and practice infection prevention measures including hand hygiene, avoiding sick contacts, and food safety precautions."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","title":"CRRT Modalities and Solute Removal Mechanisms","body":"Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy (CRRT) encompasses several modalities that differ primarily in their solute removal mechanisms. Understanding these mechanisms is essential for selecting the appropriate therapy and optimizing prescriptions.\n\nCONVECTION: This mechanism uses hydrostatic pressure to push fluid across a semipermeable membrane, dragging dissolved solutes along through 'solvent drag.' Convection is particularly effective for middle-molecule clearance. In CVVH (Continuous Veno-Venous Hemofiltration), convection is the primary clearance mechanism, requiring replacement fluid to maintain volume balance.\n\nDIFFUSION: Solutes move down their concentration gradient from blood to dialysate (or vice versa) across the membrane. Diffusion is most efficient for small molecules like urea and creatinine. CVVHD (Continuous Veno-Venous Hemodialysis) relies primarily on diffusion, using dialysate flow to create concentration gradients.\n\nCVVHDF (Continuous Veno-Venous Hemodiafiltration) combines both mechanisms, using both replacement fluid and dialysate to maximize clearance across all molecular weight ranges. This modality offers flexibility but requires managing both fluid streams.\n\nSLED (Sustained Low-Efficiency Dialysis) represents an intermediate approach—extended intermittent therapy typically run 6-12 hours with lower blood and dialysate flows than conventional hemodialysis. SLED provides better hemodynamic stability than standard HD while requiring less continuous monitoring than CRRT.\n\nSelection depends on patient hemodynamics, available resources, and specific clearance needs. All modalities require careful attention to anticoagulation, vascular access, and dose delivery."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","title":"Anticoagulation Strategies in CRRT","body":"Preventing circuit clotting while minimizing bleeding risk is a central challenge in CRRT management. Two primary anticoagulation strategies are used: systemic heparin and regional citrate anticoagulation.\n\nSYSTEMIC HEPARIN: Unfractionated heparin is administered continuously or intermittently to maintain circuit patency. Monitoring typically uses aPTT or anti-Xa levels. Advantages include familiarity, low cost, and easy reversal with protamine. Disadvantages include bleeding risk, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT), and variable response in critically ill patients.\n\nREGIONAL CITRATE ANTICOAGULATION: Citrate is infused pre-filter, chelating ionized calcium within the circuit to prevent clotting. Calcium is then replaced post-filter or systemically to maintain normal patient calcium levels. Citrate is metabolized by the liver to bicarbonate.\n\nAdvantages of citrate include: significantly reduced bleeding risk (anticoagulation is circuit-confined), longer circuit life, and no HIT risk. Disadvantages include: complexity of monitoring, risk of citrate accumulation (especially in liver failure or shock), metabolic alkalosis from citrate metabolism, and need for calcium monitoring.\n\nMonitoring citrate anticoagulation requires checking: circuit ionized calcium (target 0.25-0.4 mmol/L), systemic ionized calcium (target 1.0-1.2 mmol/L), total calcium, acid-base status, and the total-to-ionized calcium ratio (elevation >2.5 suggests accumulation).\n\nContraindications to citrate include severe liver failure, severe shock with impaired perfusion, and certain metabolic conditions. In these cases, heparin or saline flushes may be preferred.\n\nKDIGO guidelines suggest citrate as the preferred anticoagulation method when not contraindicated, based on evidence for longer circuit life and reduced bleeding."},{"topic":"Acute Therapies","title":"CRRT Dosing, Access, and Clinical Considerations","body":"Proper CRRT prescription and management requires attention to dose delivery, vascular access, and ongoing monitoring for complications.\n\nDOSE PRESCRIPTION: KDIGO guidelines recommend a delivered dose of 20-25 mL/kg/hr for critically ill patients with AKI. The dose is calculated based on effluent flow rate (total waste fluid). Importantly, prescribed dose often exceeds delivered dose by 15-30% due to treatment downtime from circuit changes, transport, procedures, and alarms. To achieve target delivered dose, consider prescribing 25-30 mL/kg/hr.\n\nVASCULAR ACCESS: The right internal jugular vein is the preferred site for CRRT catheters. It provides direct access to the superior vena cava/right atrium junction, has lower infection rates than femoral access, and avoids subclavian vein stenosis that could compromise future arteriovenous fistula creation. Femoral access is acceptable when other sites are unavailable but carries higher infection risk. Catheters should be dual-lumen, non-cuffed, designed for high flow rates (typically 11-13 French for adults).\n\nELECTROLYTE MANAGEMENT: CRRT significantly affects electrolyte balance. Potassium is effectively cleared, but rapid shifts during initiation can precipitate arrhythmias. Phosphate clearance is often inadequate, leading to hypophosphatemia during prolonged therapy—monitor and supplement as needed. Sodium correction must be gradual (no more than 10-12 mEq/L per 24 hours) to avoid neurologic complications. Bicarbonate-buffered solutions are standard, but acid-base status requires monitoring and dialysate/replacement fluid adjustments.\n\nSEPSIS-INDUCED AKI: In septic patients, CRRT initiation is based on life-threatening complications rather than arbitrary BUN/creatinine thresholds. Indications include: severe metabolic acidosis refractory to medical management, hyperkalemia with ECG changes, volume overload causing respiratory compromise, and uremic complications (pericarditis, encephalopathy, bleeding). Early initiation before severe complications develop may be considered in rapidly deteriorating patients.\n\nCIRCUIT MONITORING: Watch for rising transmembrane pressure (indicates clotting), decreasing venous pressure (access problems), and visual evidence of clotting in the circuit. Prompt intervention preserves circuit life and maintains dose delivery."},{"topic":"CKD Complications","title":"Anemia Management in Chronic Kidney Disease","body":"Anemia is one of the most common complications of CKD, affecting over 90% of patients with stage 5 disease. The primary mechanism is erythropoietin (EPO) deficiency, as damaged kidneys cannot produce adequate amounts of this hormone that stimulates red blood cell production in bone marrow.\n\nKey Assessment Points:\n- Monitor hemoglobin regularly (target: 10-11 g/dL with ESA therapy)\n- Check iron studies: ferritin, transferrin saturation (TSAT)\n- Assess for symptoms: fatigue, weakness, shortness of breath, pallor\n- Rule out other causes: blood loss, B12/folate deficiency, hemolysis\n\nTreatment Approach:\n1. Iron supplementation: Maintain ferritin >100 ng/mL (non-dialysis) or >200 ng/mL (dialysis), TSAT >20%\n2. Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents (ESAs): epoetin alfa, darbepoetin alfa\n3. Avoid hemoglobin >11.5 g/dL due to increased cardiovascular risk\n4. Consider IV iron for dialysis patients or those with poor oral absorption\n\nNursing Considerations:\n- Educate patients about iron-rich foods and ESA administration\n- Monitor blood pressure (ESAs can worsen hypertension)\n- Watch for signs of iron overload or allergic reactions to IV iron\n- Assess vascular access sites in dialysis patients receiving IV medications"},{"topic":"CKD Complications","title":"CKD-Mineral and Bone Disorder (CKD-MBD): Comprehensive Management","body":"CKD-MBD is a systemic disorder affecting mineral metabolism, bone health, and cardiovascular function. It encompasses abnormalities in calcium, phosphorus, PTH, and vitamin D metabolism that develop as kidney function declines.\n\nPathophysiology:\nAs GFR decreases, phosphate retention occurs, stimulating FGF23 release. The kidney's ability to activate vitamin D diminishes, reducing calcium absorption. Low calcium and high phosphate trigger secondary hyperparathyroidism. Over time, this leads to bone disease and vascular calcification.\n\nLaboratory Monitoring:\n- Calcium (corrected for albumin): target 8.4-9.5 mg/dL\n- Phosphate: target 2.5-4.5 mg/dL (stage 3-5), 3.5-5.5 mg/dL (dialysis)\n- Intact PTH: target 35-70 pg/mL (stage 3), 70-120 pg/mL (stage 4), 150-300 pg/mL (stage 5/dialysis)\n- Alkaline phosphatase: marker of bone turnover\n- 25-hydroxyvitamin D: maintain >30 ng/mL\n\nTreatment Strategies:\n1. Phosphate control: dietary restriction (800-1000 mg/day), phosphate binders (calcium-based, sevelamer, lanthanum)\n2. Vitamin D supplementation: cholecalciferol for deficiency, calcitriol/analogs for secondary HPT\n3. Calcimimetics: cinacalcet for refractory secondary HPT\n4. Parathyroidectomy: for severe, medication-resistant disease\n\nNursing Priorities:\n- Teach proper timing of phosphate binders (with meals)\n- Monitor for hypercalcemia symptoms (nausea, confusion, polyuria)\n- Assess for bone pain, fractures, height loss\n- Emphasize medication adherence to prevent long-term complications"},{"topic":"CKD Complications","title":"Cardiovascular Complications and Risk Reduction in CKD","body":"Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death in CKD patients, accounting for approximately 50% of mortality. CKD patients are more likely to die from cardiovascular events than progress to ESRD.\n\nUnique Cardiovascular Risks in CKD:\n- Accelerated atherosclerosis from chronic inflammation and endothelial dysfunction\n- Left ventricular hypertrophy from hypertension and anemia\n- Vascular calcification from CKD-MBD\n- Arrhythmias from electrolyte imbalances (especially hyperkalemia)\n- Uremic cardiomyopathy in advanced disease\n- Increased arterial stiffness\n\nRisk Factor Management:\n1. Blood pressure: Target <130/80 mmHg; ACE inhibitors or ARBs preferred for proteinuria\n2. Lipid management: Statins recommended for most CKD patients (except dialysis)\n3. Glycemic control: HbA1c target ~7% for diabetic patients\n4. Anemia management: Maintain hemoglobin 10-11 g/dL\n5. Mineral metabolism: Control phosphate, calcium, PTH to reduce calcification\n6. Lifestyle: Smoking cessation, weight management, physical activity as tolerated\n\nScreening Recommendations:\n- Regular blood pressure monitoring\n- Lipid panel annually (or more frequently if abnormal)\n- ECG for patients with symptoms or high risk\n- Echocardiogram if heart failure symptoms present\n- Assess for peripheral arterial disease\n\nPatient Education:\n- Recognize cardiac symptoms: chest pain, shortness of breath, palpitations, edema\n- Medication adherence for BP, lipids, diabetes\n- Dietary modifications: low sodium, heart-healthy fats, appropriate potassium/phosphate\n- Importance of regular follow-up and laboratory monitoring\n- When to seek emergency care for cardiac symptoms"}];
